U-Boot for the Ronetix i.MX8MN-COMPACT-CM board

Supported boards
================
  defconfig                        LPDDR4  Ethernet PHY
  -------------------------------  ------  ----------------------
  imx8mn_compact_cm_defconfig      1 GB    AR8031, address 0

Hardware:
  - SoC:     i.MX8M Nano (i.MX8MNL QuadLite)
  - PMIC:    ROHM BD718xx at 0x4b on I2C2
  - Console: UART2, 115200 8N1
  - SD card: USDHC2 (U-Boot mmc 1), card detect GPIO2_12
  - eMMC:    USDHC3 (U-Boot mmc 2)

Quick Start
===========
- Set the cross compiler
- Get the ARM Trusted Firmware and the DDR firmware
- Build U-Boot
- Write flash.bin to the SD card and/or the eMMC
- Boot

Set the cross compiler
======================
$ export CROSS_COMPILE=/opt/cross/aarch64-ronetix-linux-gnu-11.1.0/bin/aarch64-ronetix-linux-gnu-

Get the ARM Trusted Firmware and the DDR firmware
=================================================
From the U-Boot top directory:

$ tools/imx8m_build_firmware.sh imx8mn

The script builds BL31 from https://github.com/nxp-imx/imx-atf
(branch lf_v2.10) and copies it to bl31.bin, and gets the LPDDR4 training
firmware (lpddr4_pmu_train_*.bin) from the NXP firmware-imx-8.20 package.
Both are only downloaded if missing.

Run the script again after building U-Boot for an i.MX8MM board, since
BL31 is SoC specific. An i.MX8MM BL31 still boots on the i.MX8MN, but
power domains, suspend and CPU hotplug do not work correctly.

Build U-Boot
============
$ make imx8mn_compact_cm_defconfig
$ make

The result is flash.bin (SPL, DDR firmware, BL31 and U-Boot).

Write flash.bin to the SD card at offset 32 KiB
===============================================
$ sudo dd if=flash.bin of=/dev/sd[x] bs=1024 seek=32 conv=fsync

Note: the offset is 32 KiB on the i.MX8MN, not 33 KiB as on the i.MX8MM.

Write flash.bin to the eMMC
===========================
The eMMC is USDHC3: "mmc 2" in U-Boot, /dev/mmcblk2 in Linux.
flash.bin can be written either to the user area or to a boot partition
(boot0/boot1). The offset is 32 KiB in the user area, but 0 (the very
beginning) in a boot partition.

From U-Boot (booted from the SD card)
-------------------------------------
Load flash.bin into RAM, from the FAT partition of the SD card:
  => fatload mmc 1:1 ${loadaddr} flash.bin
or over the network:
  => tftpboot ${loadaddr} flash.bin

Calculate the size in 512-byte blocks:
  => setexpr blkcnt ${filesize} + 1ff
  => setexpr blkcnt ${blkcnt} / 200

a) User area, offset 32 KiB = block 0x40:
  => mmc dev 2 0
  => mmc write ${loadaddr} 0x40 ${blkcnt}
  => mmc partconf 2 0 7 0      (boot from the user area)

b) Boot partition boot0, offset 0 KiB = block 0:
  => mmc dev 2 1
  => mmc write ${loadaddr} 0 ${blkcnt}
  => mmc partconf 2 1 1 0      (boot from boot0, boot ACK enabled)

From Linux
----------
a) User area:
  # dd if=flash.bin of=/dev/mmcblk2 bs=1024 seek=32 conv=fsync

b) Boot partition boot0:
  # echo 0 > /sys/block/mmcblk2boot0/force_ro
  # dd if=flash.bin of=/dev/mmcblk2boot0 bs=1024 seek=0 conv=fsync
  # echo 1 > /sys/block/mmcblk2boot0/force_ro
  # mmc bootpart enable 1 1 /dev/mmcblk2   (mmc-utils)

Boot
====
Set the boot switch to SD boot or eMMC boot and power on the board.

The first time, boot from the SD card, then write flash.bin to the eMMC
as described above and switch to eMMC boot.

SPL loads U-Boot through the boot ROM API ("Trying to boot from BOOTROM").
"Boot Stage: Recovery boot" means the boot ROM could not boot from the
primary boot device (e.g. an empty eMMC) and fell back to the SD card.

Booting Linux
=============
For every MMC device (SD card first, then eMMC) U-Boot tries:

1. Distro boot from the first (FAT) partition:
   extlinux/extlinux.conf, boot.scr or EFI. This is what Yocto images use
   (UBOOT_EXTLINUX = "1").

2. Fallback, when none of these files exists: load "Image" and the device
   tree ${fdtfile} from the first partition and boot with
     console=${console} root=/dev/mmcblkXp2 rootwait rw ${optargs}
   where X is the MMC device (SD card: 1, eMMC: 2), so a card prepared by
   hand with just Image and the .dtb boots without an extlinux.conf.
   Extra kernel arguments:
     => setenv optargs "quiet"; saveenv

Memory layout: kernel_addr_r=0x42000000, fdt_addr_r=0x48000000
(room for a kernel Image of up to 96 MiB).

Notes
=====
- The MAC address fuses are not programmed, a random MAC address is used.
  Set a fixed one with:
    => setenv ethaddr xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx; saveenv
- "bad CRC, using default environment" on the first boot is expected,
  run "saveenv" once.
